.\"
.\" nroff source for blackboxai.1 man page
.\"
.\" A template (.1.in): @pkgdatadir@, @defaultmenu@, and @version@ are
.\" substituted at configure time (doc/meson.build) from the same values
.\" src/meson.build bakes into the binary, so the page and the compositor
.\" never disagree on where things live.
.\"
.\" Ported from the classic Blackbox page (Brad Hughes / bbidulock lineage,
.\" R.B. "Brig" Young's nroff) and made honest for the Wayland build.
.\"
.\" Indented preformat macro.
.de EX
.ne 5
.if n .sp 1
.if t .sp .5
.nf
.in +.5i
..
.de EE
.fi
.in -.5i
.if n .sp 1
.if t .sp .5
..
.TH blackboxai 1 "July 2026" "@version@" "BlackboxAI"
.\"
.\" * * * * * NAME * * * * *
.\"
.SH NAME
blackboxai \- a Wayland compositor in the style of the Blackbox window manager
.\"
.\" * * * * * SYNOPSIS * * * * *
.\"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blackboxai
.RI "[ \-\-headless ] [ \-rc" " rcfile " "]"
.br
Unknown arguments are ignored.
.\"
.\" * * * * * DESCRIPTION * * * * *
.\"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" ----- overview -----
BlackboxAI is a Wayland compositor that reproduces the look and behaviour of
the classic Blackbox window manager. It is a from-scratch wlroots compositor,
not an X11 port: it reads your existing
.B ~/.blackboxrc
resource file, style files, and menu files unchanged, and renders the same
toolbar, root/window/workspace menus, workspaces, and slit.
.PP
.\" ----- usage overview -----
Applications are launched from a menu opened by right-clicking the desktop
(the root surface). Workspaces are switched from the toolbar or by keybinding.
Individual windows are controlled by buttons on the title bar, and more options
are available by right-clicking the title bar.
.PP
.\" ----- design overview -----
BlackboxAI generates its window decorations on the fly from textures and
gradients. Themes, called styles in Blackbox terminology, are colours, fonts,
and texture descriptions rather than pixmaps, so there are no image
dependencies and the decoration cost stays small.
.PP
.\" ----- keybindings / workspaces overview -----
Keybindings are built in and fixed (see KEYBINDINGS), not delegated to an
external tool and not yet configurable from the resource file. Workspaces use
the built-in toolbar and workspace menu; there is no separate pager, though the
compositor does export its workspace set over the ext-workspace-v1 protocol for
an external panel that speaks it.
.PP
.\" ----- slit overview -----
The slit is an edge-of-screen strip that hosts system-tray icons. On Wayland it
speaks the StatusNotifierItem (SNI) protocol over D-Bus, not the X11 XEMBED
dock-app protocol, so classic dockapps do not run in it; a StatusNotifierItem
application (many modern tray programs) appears there instead.
.\"
.\" * * * * * OPTIONS * * * * *
.\"
.SH OPTIONS
BlackboxAI parses only the following options; any other argument is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-headless
Run on the headless wlroots backend with no real output. Intended for
development and automated tests, not for a login session.
.TP
.BI \-rc \ rcfile
Use an alternate resource file instead of
.BR ~/.blackboxrc .
.\"
.\" * * * * * STARTING AND EXITING * * * * *
.\"
.SH STARTING AND EXITING
BlackboxAI is a Wayland session. Pick
.B BlackboxAI
at your display-manager (for example GDM) greeter and log in; the session is
listed from
.I <datadir>/wayland-sessions/blackboxai.desktop
installed with the package.
.PP
On startup, BlackboxAI reads
.B ~/.blackboxrc
and uses
.B session.menuFile
to find the menu. If that key is unset or the file is missing it falls back to
.B @defaultmenu@
and, failing that, to a small built-in menu that can start a terminal and
restart or exit the compositor.
.PP
On exit, BlackboxAI writes its current configuration back to
.BR ~/.blackboxrc .
.EX 0
.B NOTE:
If ~/.blackboxrc is edited during a session, restart the compositor
("Restart" on the root menu) to apply the changes - otherwise they are
overwritten when the running session writes its state on exit.
.EE
.B Ctrl+Alt+Backspace
quits the compositor. This is the Wayland analogue of the classic X-server
kill and exists to escape a wedged session; it is suppressed while a session
lock is up (locked means locked).
.PP
.\"
.\" * * * * * USING BLACKBOXAI * * * * *
.\"
.SH USING BLACKBOXAI
.PP
Mouse buttons on the desktop (root surface):
.TP
.BR "Button Three" "  (Right Button)"
Open the root menu.
.TP
Button One and Button Two on the desktop are not bound.
.\"
.\" ***** ROOT MENU *****
.\"
.TP
.B Root Menu
The root menu launches applications and reaches the workspace and configuration
menus. Its contents come from the menu file (see MENU FILE); a starter menu
ships at
.B @defaultmenu@
and can be copied and pointed at with
.B session.menuFile
in
.BR ~/.blackboxrc .
.\"
.\" ***** WORKSPACE MENU *****
.\"
.TP
.B Workspace Menu
Reached through the root menu (a
.B [workspaces]
entry). Lists workspaces by name; selecting one switches to it. Windows on a
workspace appear in a pop-out submenu, and selecting a window switches to its
workspace and focuses it. Workspaces can be named in
.B ~/.blackboxrc
(see RESOURCE FILE).
.\"
.\" ***** THE SLIT *****
.\"
.TP
.B The Slit
The slit hosts system-tray icons that speak the StatusNotifierItem protocol
over D-Bus. Right-clicking the slit opens a menu to change its placement and to
toggle auto-hide. A tray item's own context menu is shown as text entries (no
icon column).
.\"
.\" ***** THE TOOLBAR *****
.\"
.TP
.B The Toolbar
The left of the toolbar is the workspace control, the centre shows the focused
window, and the right is a clock whose format is set by
.B session.screen<num>.strftimeFormat
(see RESOURCE FILE). Right-clicking the toolbar opens a menu to set its
placement and auto-hide.
.\"
.\" ***** WINDOW DECORATIONS *****
.\"
.TP
.B Window Decorations
A decorated window has a title bar with three buttons (iconify, maximize,
close, left to right), and a bottom handle split into two corner resize grips
and a centre move grip.
.\"
.\" ----- mouse buttons -----
.\"
.TP
.BR "Button One" "  (Left Button)"
Drag the title bar to move the window, or a corner grip to resize.
Click iconify to hide the window, maximize to maximize it, or close to close it.
.TP
.BR "Alt + Button One" ""
Drag anywhere on the window to move it.
.TP
.BR "Button Three" "  (Right Button)"
Click the title bar to open the window menu.
.TP
.\"
.\" ----- window menu -----
.\"
.B The window menu contains:
.TP
.B Send To ...
Send this window to another workspace.
.TP
.B Iconify
Hide the window; recover it from the icon menu (Super+Alt+T).
.TP
.B Maximize
Toggle window maximization.
.TP
.B Raise
Bring the window to the front and focus it.
.TP
.B Lower
Drop the window below the others.
.TP
.B Stick
Keep this window on every workspace.
.TP
.B Close
Ask the client to close.
.\"
.\" * * * * * KEYBINDINGS * * * * *
.\"
.SH KEYBINDINGS
Keybindings are built in and fixed in this release; they are not yet
configurable from
.BR ~/.blackboxrc .
.TP
.B Super+Right / Super+Left
Next / previous workspace.
.TP
.B Super+1 .. Super+4
Go to workspace 1-4.
.TP
.B Super+Space
Open the root menu.
.TP
.B Super+Q
Close the focused window.
.TP
.B Alt+Tab / Alt+Shift+Tab
Cycle windows most-recently-used, forward / backward.
.B Super+Tab
and
.B Super+Shift+Tab
are aliases.
.TP
.B Super+Alt+T
Open the icon menu (iconified windows).
.TP
.B Super+F7
Screenshot a region to the clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Alt+Backspace
Quit the compositor (escape a wedged session; suppressed while locked).
.TP
.B Super+F
Toggle fullscreen for the focused window.
.TP
.B Super+Shift+Left / Super+Shift+Right
Snap the window to the left / right half of the screen.
.TP
.B Super+Ctrl+Left / Right / Up / Down
Move the window to the output in that direction.
.\"
.\" * * * * * STYLES * * * * *
.\"
.SH STYLES
Styles are collections of colours, fonts, and textures that control the
appearance of the compositor. They are recorded in style files. The bundled
system styles are in
.I @pkgdatadir@/styles
and the default is
.BR Results .
The menu identifies a style by its file name; styles can be organised into
directories at your discretion and listed with
.B [stylesdir]
or
.B [stylesmenu]
in the menu file.
.PP
A style file is a list of resources. The honoured families are the
.B window.*
decoration textures and their colour/text/border keys, the
.BR toolbar.* ,
.BR menu.* ,
and
.B slit
families, and the top-level
.BR rootCommand ,
.BR borderWidth ,
and
.B bevelWidth
keys. A style may set the desktop background; the background is drawn natively
(the classic
.B bsetroot
gradient/solid syntax is interpreted directly). The per-key style syntax is not
documented here - copy and edit an existing style file.
.PP
.B Security note:
a
.B rootCommand
in
.B ~/.blackboxrc
is run through
.BR /bin/sh .
A
.B rootCommand
in a style file is
.I not
run through a shell (a downloaded theme should not run arbitrary commands).
.\"
.\" * * * * * MENU FILE * * * * *
.\"
.SH MENU FILE
The starter menu is installed at
.BR @defaultmenu@ .
Copy it into your home directory, point
.B session.menuFile
in
.B ~/.blackboxrc
at the copy, and edit it. Menu commands follow this general form:
.PP
.BI "[command]" "  (label|filename) {shell command|filename}"
.\"
.\" ----- menu commands -----
.\"
.TP
.BI "   #    " "string..."
A hash starts a comment, whole-line or trailing.
.TP
.BI "[begin]" "  (string)"
Opens the menu.
.I string
is the title shown at the top.
.TP
.B [end]
Closes the menu file or a submenu block.
.TP
.BI "[exec]" "  (label) {command}"
Run
.I command
through the shell.
.TP
.BR "[nop]" " (label)"
An inert, disabled entry - a labelled spacer.
.TP
.BR "[separator]" " (or [sep])"
A separator line.
.TP
.BI "[submenu]" "  (label) {title}"
Open a submenu named
.IR label .
The optional
.I title
is accepted but not stored - the cascade is titled by the label.
.TP
.BI "[include]" "  (filename)"
Insert
.I filename
at this point (it should not contain its own
.BR [begin] / [end] ).
.B Pipe menus are not supported:
an
.B [include] |command
form is skipped with a diagnostic rather than run, because reading a
subprocess at menu-open would stall the compositor.
.TP
.BI "[style]" "  (label) {filename}"
Apply the style file
.I filename
when selected.
.TP
.BI "[stylesdir]" "  (path)"
List the style files in
.I path
inline in the menu.
.TP
.BI "[stylesmenu]" "  (label) {path}"
List the style files in
.I path
inside a submenu named
.IR label .
.TP
.BI "[workspaces]" "  (label)"
Insert the workspace menu.
.TP
.BI "[config]" "  (label)"
Insert the configuration menu (focus model, placement, toolbar and slit
options).
.TP
.BI "[reconfig]" "  (label)"
Reread the configuration and reload.
.B A trailing {command} is ignored
- the classic page promised it would run first, but classic never ran it
either, so it is dropped with a diagnostic here.
.TP
.BI "[restart]" "  (label) {command}"
Restart the compositor. With
.I command
it execs the named window manager instead. Either way every client is torn
down first - the compositor is the display server (see DEVIATIONS).
.TP
.BI "[exit]" "  (label)"
Exit the compositor, ending the Wayland session.
.EX
.B A working example (close to the shipped starter menu):
[begin] (BlackboxAI)
   [exec] (Terminal) {kitty}
   [exec] (Run...) {fuzzel}
   [separator]
   [submenu] (Styles) {Pick a theme}
      [stylesdir] (@pkgdatadir@/styles)
   [end]
   [config] (Configuration)
   [workspaces] (Workspaces)
   [separator]
   [restart] (Restart)
   [exit] (Exit)
[end]
.EE
.\"
.\" * * * * * RESOURCE FILE * * * * *
.\"
.SH RESOURCE FILE
.BI "$HOME" "/.blackboxrc"
.PP
These keys are read from
.B ~/.blackboxrc
at startup. Some are also set from the configuration menu, which writes the one
changed key back to the file in place. As with the classic manager, edits made
while a session is running take effect only after a restart, since the running
session writes its state on exit.
.PP
Keys marked
.I screen<num>
are per-screen;
.I <num>
is the screen number, default 0.
.TP 3
.BI "session.styleFile" "  [filepath]"
Path to the current style file. Default is the bundled
.BR Results .
.TP 3
.BI "session.menuFile" "  [filepath]"
Path to the menu file. Default is
.BR @defaultmenu@ .
.TP 3
.BI "rootCommand" "  [command]"
A command run through
.B /bin/sh
at startup (typically to set the desktop background).
.TP 3
.BI "session.focusModel" "  SEE BELOW"
.B SloppyFocus
(focus follows the mouse; the default) can be modified with
.B AutoRaise
(raise after
.BR session.autoRaiseDelay )
or
.B ClickRaise
(raise on a click in the client area).
.B ClickToFocus
requires a click to focus and raise and cannot be combined with the modifiers.
.EX
.B Default is SloppyFocus.
[SloppyFocus [AutoRaise] [ClickRaise] | ClickToFocus]
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.focusNewWindows" "  [True|False]"
Give focus to a newly mapped window.
.EX
.B Default is True.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.autoRaiseDelay" "  [integer]"
Milliseconds before an auto-raise or auto-hide fires.
.EX
.B Default is 400.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.doubleClickInterval" "  [integer]"
Maximum milliseconds between two clicks to count as a double click.
.EX
.B Default is 250.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.changeWorkspaceWithMouseWheel" "  [True|False]"
Scrolling on the desktop switches workspaces.
.EX
.B Default is True.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.toolbarActionsWithMouseWheel" "  [True|False]"
Scrolling on the toolbar switches workspaces.
.EX
.B Default is True.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.windowPlacement" "  SEE BELOW"
Where new windows are placed.
.EX
.B Default is RowSmart.
[RowSmart | ColSmart | Center | Cascade]
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.workspaces" "  [integer]"
Number of workspaces.
.EX
.B Default is 4.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.workspaceNames" "  [string[, string...]]"
Comma-separated workspace names; extra workspaces are named "Workspace N".
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.strftimeFormat" "  [string]"
A
.BR strftime (3)
format string for the toolbar clock, applied live.
.EX
.B Default is %I:%M %p
(12-hour clock with an am/pm indicator).
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.toolbar.widthPercent" "  [1-100]"
Percentage of screen width used by the toolbar.
.EX
.B Default is 66.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.toolbar.placement" "  SEE BELOW"
Toolbar screen position.
.EX
[  TopLeft  |   TopCenter  |   TopRight  |
 BottomLeft | BottomCenter | BottomRight ]
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.toolbar.autoHide" "  [True|False]"
Hide the toolbar when not in use.
.EX
.B Default is False.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.toolbar.onTop" "  [True|False]"
Read but inert: the value is parsed, but there is no menu toggle and no layer
restack, so it does not currently change stacking.
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.slit.placement" "  SEE BELOW"
Slit screen position.
.EX
[  TopLeft  |   TopCenter  |   TopRight  |
 CenterLeft |              | CenterRight |
 BottomLeft | BottomCenter | BottomRight ]
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.slit.direction" "  [Horizontal|Vertical]"
Slit growth direction.
.EX
.B Default is Vertical.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.slit.autoHide" "  [True|False]"
Hide the slit when not in use.
.EX
.B Default is False.
.EE
.TP 3
.BI "session.screen<num>.slit.onTop" "  [True|False]"
Read but inert, as with the toolbar.
.\"
.\" * * * * * ENVIRONMENT * * * * *
.\"
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Used to find
.B ~/.blackboxrc
and the user config directory.
.TP
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
When set, BlackboxAI runs nested inside the named parent Wayland compositor
(useful for development).
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the location of
.B ~/.config
for the autostart scan.
.\"
.\" * * * * * FILES * * * * *
.\"
.SH FILES
.TP
.B blackboxai
The compositor binary.
.TP
.B ~/.blackboxrc
Per-user resource file.
.TP
.B @defaultmenu@
The default menu file.
.TP
.I @pkgdatadir@/styles
The bundled system styles (default
.BR Results ).
.TP
.I <datadir>/wayland-sessions/blackboxai.desktop
The session entry the display manager lists.
.TP
.B ~/.config/autostart
.PD 0
.TP
.B /etc/xdg/autostart
.PD
Scanned at startup under
.BR XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=Blackbox ;
a matching
.B .desktop
file is launched.
.\"
.\" * * * * * DEVIATIONS FROM CLASSIC BLACKBOX * * * * *
.\"
.SH DEVIATIONS FROM CLASSIC BLACKBOX
This build differs from the X11 Blackbox in ways that follow from being a
Wayland compositor rather than a window manager:
.TP
.B Restart and exit tear down every client.
The compositor is the display server, so
.B [restart]
and
.B [exit]
take every window with them - there is no separate X server to outlive them.
.TP
.B No XWayland.
X11-only applications do not run in this release (deferred).
.TP
.B No window shade.
The xdg-shell protocol has no shade concept. (The bundled
.B Shade
style is a theme name, not the feature.)
.TP
.B The slit is a tray, not a dock.
It hosts StatusNotifierItem tray icons over D-Bus, not X11 XEMBED dockapps.
.TP
.B Tray context menus are text-only.
A tray item's menu is shown as text entries, with no icon column.
.TP
.B onTop keys are inert.
.B toolbar.onTop
and
.B slit.onTop
are parsed but there is no menu toggle and no restack.
.TP
.B Keybindings are fixed.
They are built in and not yet configurable from the resource file.
.\"
.\" * * * * * AUTHORS * * * * *
.\"
.SH AUTHORS
BlackboxAI is a rewrite in the style of the bbidulock/blackboxwm line of
Blackbox, originally designed and written by Brad Hughes. The classic manual
page this one is derived from was written by R.B. "Brig" Young.
.\"
.\" * * * * * SEE ALSO * * * * *
.\"
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strftime (3)
.PP
Project page:
.B https://github.com/hila-shemer/blackboxai
